# AO7 lane failure - infrastructure, not lane logic

- Lane: AO7, stamp 20261012T135739Z, role VERIFY/SECURITY, checker model gpt-6.1-sol (openai, OAuth,
  reasoning medium), route openai.
- Preflight: `PREFLIGHT_CLEAN HEAD=8924aaa`.
- Child exit: **1**. Wrapper exit: **1**. Postflight: `dirty=0 HEAD=e83051a`.
- **No receipt was written** (`RECEIPT_WRITTEN` absent). That is the launcher behaving as designed: a
  failed child must not produce a receipt, and the wrapper must not return 0 over it.
- Outputs in `verify/ao7/`: **none**. The lane produced no verdict artefact.

## Cause

The provider refused the lane's request at the request level:

```
ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try rephrasing your
request. If you're doing authorized security work that requires more cyber permissive safeguards, apply
for Daybreak access via https://platform.openai.com/settings/organization/status-and-access before retrying.
```

twice, followed by `failed to record rollout items: thread 01a13067-... not found` and `tokens used
57,143`, then exit 1. This is an upstream content filter on the request the lane was about to send, not a
[credential-material reference redacted]
[credential-material reference redacted]
environment dump it had collected while testing whether an environment variable could widen the harness.

## Work the lane did complete before it was killed (from the retained log)

The log is preserved in `stage-b/AO7_20261012T135739Z.log`. It reached real M1-M9 re-runs, for example:

```
===== M8 =====
COMMAND: bash "$G" EXECUTE brief_nt_a.md
ASSIGNMENT role=EXECUTE model=opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash provider=opencode-go effort=high route=catalog
QUALIFICATION_SKIP_UNAUTHORISED: GATE_SKIP_QUALIFY=1 needs GATE_SKIP_QUALIFY_ACK.md naming role EXECUTE
GATE_REFUSED_NO_WRITERS_STARTED
SUBJECT_EXIT=6
```

and, elsewhere, `LANE_SET_OK lanes=1 model=... route=catalog` with `SUBJECT_EXIT=0`. Those observations are
**not** an adjudication: the lane never assembled a verdict, so nothing here may be reported as one. They
show only that the lane had begun reproducing the counterexamples independently.

## Consequence for the record

Round 7 has **no verdict**. The question it was asked is unanswered. A re-run (AO8) was launched with a
[credential-material reference redacted]
request is refused rather than silently retrying the same content.
DOCUMENT verify/harness/HARNESS_DEFECT_LOG.md
# Harness defect log - round S4POST-20261012

Every defect below was found by running something, not by reading the code. Two of them were found by
the independent officer; two by using the new tools for real; one is a **false positive I produced and
then had to retract**. Kept because a harness that reports its own errors is the only kind worth
trusting, and because the retraction is the most instructive entry here.

## H1 - the handover was dirty by construction (found by the round-3 officer, verdict NOT_CLOSED)

- **Symptom**: every officer was handed a worktree carrying the harness's own brief, runner and lane log.
- **Cause**: the harness wrote its artifacts into the very worktree it then handed to the officer.
- **Fix**: `run_lane.sh` commits harness artifacts first, refuses to launch against a dirty worktree, and
  writes the lane log outside the worktree.
- **Evidence**: the officer independently confirmed the substantive closure (every path in the closure
  commits is under `.hgk/rounds/`, no code path changed) and rejected only the cleanliness promise.

## H2 - the launcher failed open three ways (found by the round-4 officer, verdict PARTIAL)

| ID | Demonstrated defect | Fix | Test |
|---|---|---|---|
| D1 | a missing brief and a failed log copy were masked; the wrapper exited 0 while its own child had failed, and still committed a message claiming the log had been copied in | validate the brief before launch; check the copy; state in the commit message whether a log was really copied; propagate the child status | T2, T5 |
| D2 | postflight detected dirt, printed `POSTFLIGHT:dirty=1`, and still exited 0 | detected dirt is a failure (exit 5) | T4 |
| D3 | `mkdir -p "$WT/$OUTSUB"` created the officer output directory at the **repository root**, outside the round's write set, so the round-relative fallback never ran | round-relative only, and `..`/absolute paths refused | T3 |

It also recorded that `TMPDIR` was never validated against the worktree; now refused (T6).

## H3 - the containment check could not fire (found while testing H2's fix)

- **Symptom**: T6 failed; a log directory inside the worktree was accepted.
- **Cause**: `pwd -W 2>/dev/null || pwd` printed **both** forms on this host - the `-W` form on stdout and
  then, because that form still returned nonzero, the plain form as well. The captured value carried two
  lines, so the `"$WTRES"/*` prefix test could never match.
- **Fix**: one normalising resolver (`cd ... && pwd -P`, lowercased) for both sides, with an explicit
  refusal when either side cannot be resolved.
- **Test**: T6 now passes and would fail again if the resolver regressed.

## H4 - the qualification log overwrote itself (found while using the gate)

- **Symptom**: two qualifications in the same wall-clock second wrote the same path; the second destroyed
  the first one's evidence, which is exactly the evidence I needed.
- **Fix**: the name carries role, sanitised model and pid.
- **Consequence**: the first EXECUTE qualification's raw log is **lost**, and it was not recoverable from
  anywhere else. It is not reconstructed or paraphrased anywhere in this round.

## H5 - the false positive, and the retraction (the important one)

- **What I did**: built the admission gate with the invocation `-m <model> -c model_provider="opencode-go"`.
- **What happened**: `Error: Model provider 'opencode-go' not found`. The gate reported
  `QUALIFICATION_FAILED` and refused, so it looked like the gate had reproduced the original lane failure.
- **What I claimed on that basis**: that the gate independently reproduced the assigned lane's failure,
  and that the assigned model cannot survive a two-tool round trip.
- **Why that claim was wrong**: `opencode-go/...` is a **model catalog slug**, not a provider id. The
  invocation the writers actually used is `-c model=<slug>` together with `openai_base_url` and
  `model_catalog_json`, with no `model_provider`. My gate therefore tested **my own misconfiguration**.
  The refusal was not evidence about the model at all.
- **Correction**: the route is now declared per role in `ROUND_ASSIGNMENT.json`, and both the gate and the
  launcher build the invocation from it, so the qualification and the writers use the identical route.
- **Result after the correction**: the assigned model
  (`opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash`, effort `high`, catalog route) **PASSES** the two-tool round trip -
  `QUALIFICATION_PASSED`, `EXECUTE_QUALIFY_EXIT=0`, transcript at
  `verify/harness/qualification_logs/`.
- **The lesson, stated plainly**: a gate that fails closed on its own misconfiguration is worse than no
  gate, because it manufactures authoritative-looking refusals. Fail-closed protects against a bad lane;
  it does not protect against a bad test. Any refusal must name the exact configuration it refused, and a
  refusal that the operator cannot reproduce by hand is a harness bug until proven otherwise.

### H5 resolution

After the route was corrected the gate was run again requiring three consecutive passes. The assigned
lane passed attempt 1 and was rejected at attempt 2 with the original 400; the checker lane passed 3 of 3.
So the gate refuses the assigned lane, for a reason it measured rather than one it misconfigured - and
Correction 1 in `ACCEPTANCE_RECORD_STAGEB.md` over-corrected in the other direction and is itself
superseded by Correction 2. See `QUALIFICATION_FINDINGS.md`.

## H6 - two test-harness bugs of my own (cosmetic, recorded for completeness)

- the gate test helper built its command with a bare `"$@"` expansion of `NAME=VALUE` assignments, so every
  case exited 127; `env` is required.
- the receipt-isolation case re-used brief names from an earlier case, whose committed receipt made the
  assertion vacuous; it now uses unique brief names.
DOCUMENT verify/harness/QUALIFICATION_FINDINGS.md
# Qualification findings: what actually breaks, and how often

Measured with the corrected route (`-c model=<slug>` plus `openai_base_url` and
`model_catalog_json`, no `model_provider`), the identical no-write qualification brief, and the identical
CLI. Raw transcripts: `C:\Users\user\AppData\Local\Temp\qual-repeat\`. Raw run text:
`QUALIFICATION_FLAKINESS.txt`, `QUALIFICATION_MATRIX.txt`.

## The rates

| Configuration | Passes | Two-tool round trips attempted |
|---|---|---|
| assigned model `opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash` at effort `high` | 3 | 6 |
| assigned model `opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash` at effort `none` | 2 | 6 |
| backup model `opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash` at effort `high` | 6 | 6 |
| checker model `gpt-6.1-sol` at effort `medium`, openai route | 1 | 1 |

Six attempts per configuration is a small sample. These are counts, not reliabilities; do not read
"3 of 6" as "50% reliable".

## What the failure is, exactly

The assigned model's failing runs are **not** a failure to use tools. In
`r_opencode-go_deepseek-v4.1-flash_high_1.log` both shell calls succeeded (the second in 547 ms) and the
rejection then landed on the **next** request:

```
Provider error 400: Upstream request failed: [invalid_request_error]
The `reasoning_content` in the thinking mode must be passed back to the API.
```

`tokens used 479`. So the trigger is not transcript size, not the first turn, and not tool use as such.
It is the continuation request **after a second tool result**, in thinking mode.

## The websocket 426 is exonerated by the same data

Every one of the 18 repeat logs contains exactly one
`ERROR codex_api::endpoint::responses_websocket: failed to connect to websocket: HTTP error: 426 Upgrade
Required`, including **all** the passing runs, including the 6/6 clean backup column. The local relay
refuses the websocket and the client falls back to HTTP; that is routine here, and it is not the failure.
This is direct positive evidence for the FAR study's refutation of the 426 hypothesis.

## Axis separation

- **Model axis**: at the same effort (`high`) and the same route, the assigned model passed 3 of 6 and the
  backup model passed 6 of 6. The model is the differentiating factor in this sample.
- **Effort axis**: the assigned model failed at both `high` (3 of 6) and `none` (2 of 6). Changing the
  effort does not rescue it, so the operator's original mixed change (model **and** effort) was not
  required to escape the failure. This is the control the FAR study recorded as NOT_SEPARABLE; it is now
  separable, and the effort axis is not the cause.

## Consequence for the gate, and the repair

A single qualification is a sample of an intermittent fault, so one pass proves very little: the failure
appeared on roughly half of attempts. `run_lanes_gated.sh` now requires **`GATE_QUALIFY_RUNS`
consecutive passes (default 3)** and aborts the lane set on the first failed attempt. With the measured
per-attempt failure rate, one run would have admitted the lane on about half of attempts; three
consecutive passes make that far less likely, and each additional required pass lowers it further.

The honest limit: this raises the cost of a false admission, it does not eliminate it, and the required
count should be raised where a false admission is expensive. The gate is a filter, not a proof.

## What is still not explained

- **Why it is intermittent.** Nothing here identifies whether the adapter drops `reasoning_content` under a
  race, a retry, or a specific interleaving. The upstream message is a requirement statement, not a cause.
- **What the upstream model actually was.** Still unauthenticated. A flaky lane is not evidence of a
  substituted model, and a healthy lane is not evidence of the assigned one.
- The mechanistic lead from the FAR study - the slug being absent from the relay and registry
  reasoning-content preservation lists - remains untested, and is now the most specific thing to test,
  because a list-driven gate would explain an intermittent, model-specific, size-independent failure.

## The gate demonstrated on the real lanes

One invocation of the gate, requiring 3 consecutive passes, against both roles at the same moment:

```
EXECUTE  model=opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash effort=high
  qualification attempt 1/3 PASS
QUALIFICATION_FAILED ... attempt=2/3 exit=1
GATE_REFUSED_NO_WRITERS_STARTED          EXECUTE_QUALIFY_EXIT=7

VERIFY   model=gpt-6.1-sol effort=medium
  qualification attempt 1/3 PASS
  qualification attempt 2/3 PASS
  qualification attempt 3/3 PASS
QUALIFICATION_PASSED (consecutive_runs=3, two tool round trips each)   VERIFY_QUALIFY_EXIT=0
```

This is the point of the gate: the intermittency shows up **inside** a single gate invocation - attempt 1
clean, attempt 2 rejected - and the flaky lane is refused before any writer starts, while the sound lane is
admitted. Record: `REAL_QUALIFICATION_3CONSEC.txt`.
DOCUMENT AO5_RESULT.md
# AO5 RESULT SUMMARY - round 5, independent handover adjudication

Verdict artifact: `verify/ao5/AO_VERDICT_HANDOVER_R5.md` (70,106 bytes, 128 evidence files).
Lane: `AO5_20261012T130513Z`, checker `gpt-6.1-sol` / provider `openai` (OAuth) / reasoning `medium` -
the declared VERIFY/SECURITY model. Launched by the governed launcher itself, which reported
`PREFLIGHT_CLEAN HEAD=c93a41f` and `POSTFLIGHT:dirty=0 HEAD=f43c130`.

## The verdicts

| | Round 3 | Round 4 | Round 5 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Adjudication A (handover) | NOT_CLOSED | PARTIAL | **STILL_NOT_CLOSED** |
| Adjudication B (enforcement) | - | FAIL on 3 demonstrated branches | **real on the repaired branches, but still fails open** |

AO5's own ceiling statement: local stub control-flow verification on this host and subject; no model was
launched; "a stub proves control flow and proves nothing about a model".

## Why A is STILL_NOT_CLOSED, and it is the maker's fault

The one untracked path present before any officer evidence existed was the **launcher's own lane
manifest**, which v2 wrote into `stage-b/` *after* its clean preflight. The dispatch is now `AO5`-clean of
the officer, so the residual dirt was produced by the harness, not by the officer and not by stale maker
work. **Fixed**: the manifest is written outside the worktree and copied in at postflight, like the lane
log. This entry is not evidence that the fix works; only a later independent round can be.

## Adjudication B: five newly demonstrated fail-open paths, and their disposition

| ID | What AO5 demonstrated | Status |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | an exit-receipt copy failure returned 0 and was only a printed note | **fixed** - a failed receipt copy is fatal (exit 6); T7 |
| R2 | a **failed** `git status` produced empty output, was read as `0` paths, and the wrapper printed `PREFLIGHT_CLEAN` / `POSTFLIGHT:dirty=0` | **fixed** - a nonzero status query is fatal (exit 2); T8 |
| R3 | an already staged **outside-round** file was committed and admitted by the preflight | **fixed** - any staged path outside the round is refused (exit 6); T9 |
| R4 | `GATE_QUALIFY_RUNS=0` invoked no qualification at all yet printed `QUALIFICATION_PASSED ... consecutive_runs=0` and `LANE_SET_OK` | **fixed** - the count must be a positive integer or the gate refuses (exit 3); G8 |
| R5 | the gate qualified EXECUTE through the catalog route but launched the writers through the **default** route - it validated one lane and started another | **fixed** - the role's route is exported to the children, and G9 asserts the child's argv contains the catalog slug and no provider id |

All five are genuine. None was a hypothetical: each came with a command and its raw output. The maker's
previous claim of "universal fail-closed enforcement" is withdrawn; what is claimed now is exactly the
branches covered by T1-T9 and G1-G9, and nothing wider.

## Still open, stated so it is not mistaken for closed

- **Closure is still not independently confirmed.** Three consecutive rounds have each found real
  defects. The loop is converging and is not finished. A round 6 would be required to test these fixes.
- AO5's note that "ordinary output placement is not a physical containment proof" is not rebutted.
- AO5 could not clone the delivery from its sandbox identity
  (`dubious ownership`: lane user `CodexSandboxOffline` vs repo owner `user`), so several attacks were
  run against an external clone it prepared differently. Its report says so itself.
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Negative + positive tests for the governed lane launcher (v2).
#
# They reproduce the three defects an independent officer demonstrated against v1
# (round 4, verdict PARTIAL) and assert the repaired behaviour, plus the
# successful-run log-copy branch that officer recorded as NOT_EXECUTED.
#
# Everything runs in an EXTERNAL clone checked out at the delivered HEAD; the
# delivery worktree is never modified. The Codex binary is replaced by a stub so
# the tests are deterministic, instant, and cost no model calls.
set -u
WT="${LANE_WT:?LANE_WT must be the delivery worktree}"
# Native forward-slash path on purpose: git and other native binaries do NOT
# translate MSYS paths, so a bare /tmp/... would land under a literal C:\tmp.
BASE="${NT_BASE:-C:/Users/user/AppData/Local/Temp/nt-launcher}"
CLONE="$BASE/clone"; LOGD="$BASE/logs"
rm -rf "$BASE"; mkdir -p "$LOGD"
SHA="$(git -C "$WT" rev-parse HEAD)" || { echo "HEAD_UNRESOLVED"; exit 9; }
git clone -q --no-hardlinks "$WT" "$CLONE" 2>/dev/null || { echo "CLONE_FAILED"; exit 9; }
git -C "$CLONE" checkout -q "$SHA" || { echo "CHECKOUT_FAILED"; exit 9; }
git -C "$CLONE" config user.email nt@local.invalid; git -C "$CLONE" config user.name nt
REL=".hgk/rounds/S4POST-20261012-postrepair-recheck-and-triple-upgrade"
L="$CLONE/$REL/stage-b/run_lane.sh"
[ -f "$L" ] || { echo "LAUNCHER_NOT_IN_CLONE"; exit 9; }

STUB="$BASE/stub-codex.sh"
cat > "$STUB" <<'STUBEOF'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
echo "stub lane speaking: argv=$*"
if [ "${STUB_DIRT:-0}" = "1" ]; then printf 'injected dirt\n' > "$NT_WT/NT_POST_DIRT.txt"; fi
if [ "${STUB_ESCAPE:-0}" = "1" ]; then printf 'outside the round\n' > "$NT_WT/outside-the-round.txt"; fi
exit "${STUB_EXIT:-0}"
STUBEOF
chmod +x "$STUB"

pass=0; fail=0
ck() { if [ "$2" = "$3" ]; then echo "PASS $1 (exit=$3)"; pass=$((pass+1)); else echo "FAIL $1 expected=$2 actual=$3"; fail=$((fail+1)); fi; }
cknot() { if [ -e "$2" ]; then echo "FAIL $3 (exists: $2)"; fail=$((fail+1)); else echo "PASS $3"; pass=$((pass+1)); fi; }
ckeq() { if [ -e "$2" ]; then echo "PASS $3"; pass=$((pass+1)); else echo "FAIL $3 (missing: $2)"; fail=$((fail+1)); fi; }
has() { if grep -q "$2" "$3" 2>/dev/null; then echo "PASS $1"; pass=$((pass+1)); else echo "FAIL $1"; fail=$((fail+1)); fi; }
run() { TMPDIR="$LOGD" LANE_WT="$CLONE" CODEX_BIN="$STUB" STUB_EXIT="${1:-0}" STUB_DIRT="${2:-0}" STUB_ESCAPE="${3:-0}" NT_WT="$CLONE" bash "$L" "$4" "$5" "$6" >"$LOGD/$4.out" 2>&1; echo $?; }

echo "### T1 dirty preflight is refused (v1 passed this; regression guard)"
touch "$CLONE/NT_DIRT.txt"
ck T1_DIRTY_REFUSED 2 "$(run 0 0 0 NT1 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
grep -q 'REFUSED\[2\]' "$LOGD/NT1.out" && { echo PASS T1_says_refused; pass=$((pass+1)); } || { echo FAIL T1_says_refused; fail=$((fail+1)); }
rm -f "$CLONE/NT_DIRT.txt"

echo "### T2 missing brief: D1a - refuse BEFORE launch, never fail open"
ck T2_MISSING_BRIEF_REFUSED 3 "$(run 0 0 0 NT2 DOES_NOT_EXIST.md verify/nt)"
if ls "$LOGD"/hgk-lanes/NT2_*.log >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo "FAIL T2_no_lane_log_written"; fail=$((fail+1)); else echo "PASS T2_no_lane_log_written"; pass=$((pass+1)); fi
if grep -q 'stub lane speaking' "$LOGD/NT2.out"; then echo "FAIL T2_child_was_launched"; fail=$((fail+1)); else echo "PASS T2_child_never_launched"; pass=$((pass+1)); fi

echo "### T3 successful run: log really copied in, commit says so, no stray root dir (D3)"
ck T3_STUB_SUCCESS 0 "$(run 0 0 0 NT3 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
ls "$CLONE/$REL/stage-b/" | grep -q '^NT3_.*\.log$' && { echo PASS T3_log_copied_in; pass=$((pass+1)); } || { echo FAIL T3_log_copied_in; fail=$((fail+1)); }
# v3 makes two postflight commits (lane output, then the per-launch receipt), so the
# assertion must look past the receipt commit rather than only at HEAD.
git -C "$CLONE" log -2 --pretty=%B | grep -qi 'copied in from outside' && { echo PASS T3_commit_states_copy; pass=$((pass+1)); } || { echo FAIL T3_commit_states_copy; fail=$((fail+1)); }
ls "$CLONE/$REL/stage-b/NT3_"*.receipt >/dev/null 2>&1 && { echo PASS T3_per_launch_receipt_written; pass=$((pass+1)); } || { echo FAIL T3_per_launch_receipt_written; fail=$((fail+1)); }
cknot "x" "$CLONE/verify" "D3_no_output_dir_at_repo_root"
ckeq x "$CLONE/$REL/verify/nt" "D3_output_dir_is_round_relative"

echo "### T4 postflight dirt is REJECTED, not merely printed (D2)"
ck T4_POSTFLIGHT_DIRT_REJECTED 5 "$(run 0 1 0 NT4 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
grep -q 'POSTFLIGHT:dirty=1' "$LOGD/NT4.out" && { echo PASS T4_dirt_was_detected; pass=$((pass+1)); } || { echo FAIL T4_dirt_was_detected; fail=$((fail+1)); }
rm -f "$CLONE/NT_POST_DIRT.txt"; git -C "$CLONE" checkout -q -- . 2>/dev/null; rm -f "$CLONE/outside-the-round.txt"

echo "### T5 a non-zero child is propagated, never advertised as success"
ck T5_CHILD_EXIT_PROPAGATED 7 "$(run 7 0 0 NT5 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
grep -q 'NT5_LANE_DONE' "$LOGD/NT5.out" && { echo PASS T5_lane_reports_done; pass=$((pass+1)); } || { echo FAIL T5_lane_reports_done; fail=$((fail+1)); }

echo "### T6 TMPDIR resolving inside the worktree is refused (F)"
TMPDIR="$CLONE/tmp-inside" LANE_WT="$CLONE" CODEX_BIN="$STUB" bash "$L" NT6 brief_nt.md verify/nt >"$LOGD/NT6.out" 2>&1; c=$?
ck T6_TMPDIR_INSIDE_REFUSED 3 "$c"

echo "### T7 a failed receipt copy is fatal, not a note (R1)"
(
  cp() { local last="${@: -1}"; case "$last" in *.exit) return 23;; *) command cp "$@";; esac; }
  export -f cp
  env TMPDIR="$LOGD" LANE_WT="$CLONE" CODEX_BIN="$STUB" bash "$L" NT7 brief_nt.md verify/nt
) >"$LOGD/T7.out" 2>&1; c=$?
ck T7_RECEIPT_COPY_FATAL 6 "$c"
has T7_names_the_receipt POSTFLIGHT_RECEIPT_COPY_FAILED "$LOGD/T7.out"

echo "### T8 a FAILED git status is not a clean worktree (R2)"
(
  git() { if [ "${1:-}" = "status" ]; then return 128; fi; command git "$@"; }
  export -f git
  env TMPDIR="$LOGD" LANE_WT="$CLONE" CODEX_BIN="$STUB" bash "$L" NT8 brief_nt.md verify/nt
) >"$LOGD/T8.out" 2>&1; c=$?
ck T8_STATUS_FAILURE_REFUSED 2 "$c"
has T8_says_status_failed "git status failed" "$LOGD/T8.out"
if grep -q "stub lane speaking" "$LOGD/T8.out"; then echo "FAIL T8_child_was_launched"; fail=$((fail+1)); else echo "PASS T8_child_never_launched"; pass=$((pass+1)); fi

echo "### T9 an already staged OUTSIDE-round file is refused (R3)"
echo "outside" > "$CLONE/outside-round-fixture.txt"
git -C "$CLONE" add outside-round-fixture.txt
ck T9_STAGED_OUTSIDE_REFUSED 6 "$(run 0 0 0 NT9 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
has T9_names_the_outside_path STAGED_OUTSIDE_ROUND_REFUSED "$LOGD/NT9.out"
if git -C "$CLONE" log -1 --pretty=%s | grep -q "outside-round-fixture"; then
  echo "FAIL T9_outside_file_was_committed"; fail=$((fail+1))
else echo "PASS T9_outside_file_was_not_committed"; pass=$((pass+1)); fi
git -C "$CLONE" reset -q 2>/dev/null; rm -f "$CLONE/outside-round-fixture.txt"

echo "### T10 the manifest is really copied into the round (M1)"
# Check for THIS run's manifest by its own stamp. An earlier version of this test deleted
# every committed manifest first, which dirtied the clone and broke later cases.
ck T10_MANIFEST_RUN 0 "$(run 0 0 0 NT10 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
T10ST="$(grep -o 'NT10_LANE=[0-9TZ]*' "$LOGD/NT10.out" | head -1 | cut -d= -f2)"
if [ -n "$T10ST" ] && [ -f "$CLONE/$REL/stage-b/NT10_${T10ST}.manifest" ]; then
  echo "PASS T10_manifest_present_in_round"; pass=$((pass+1))
else echo "FAIL T10_manifest_present_in_round (stamp=$T10ST)"; fail=$((fail+1)); fi

echo "### T11 a regex-spelling alias of the round is refused (M2)"
mkdir -p "$CLONE/Xhgk/rounds/S4POST-20261012-postrepair-recheck-and-triple-upgrade"
echo outside > "$CLONE/Xhgk/rounds/S4POST-20261012-postrepair-recheck-and-triple-upgrade/outside.txt"
git -C "$CLONE" add Xhgk
ck T11_ALIAS_PREFIX_REFUSED 6 "$(run 0 0 0 NT11 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
has T11_names_the_outside_path STAGED_OUTSIDE_ROUND_REFUSED "$LOGD/NT11.out"
git -C "$CLONE" reset -q 2>/dev/null; rm -rf "$CLONE/Xhgk"

echo "### T12 a FAILED index enumeration is refused, not read as nothing staged (M3)"
(
  git() { if [ "${1:-}" = "diff" ]; then return 128; fi; command git "$@"; }
  export -f git
  env TMPDIR="$LOGD" LANE_WT="$CLONE" CODEX_BIN="$STUB" bash "$L" NT12 brief_nt.md verify/nt
) >"$LOGD/T12.out" 2>&1; c=$?
ck T12_STAGED_QUERY_FAILURE_REFUSED 6 "$c"
has T12_names_the_query STAGED_QUERY_FAILED_REFUSED "$LOGD/T12.out"

echo "### T13 dirt created by the final commit is detected (M4)"
HOOK="$CLONE/.git/hooks/post-commit"
printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\necho dirt > "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/T13_HOOK_DIRT.txt"\n' > "$HOOK"
chmod +x "$HOOK"
ck T13_POST_COMMIT_DIRT_REJECTED 5 "$(run 0 0 0 NT13 brief_nt.md verify/nt)"
has T13_names_the_dirt POSTFLIGHT_DIRTY_REJECTED "$LOGD/NT13.out"
rm -f "$HOOK" "$CLONE/T13_HOOK_DIRT.txt"

echo "### T14 a route that disagrees with the assignment is refused (M7)"
LANE_ROLE=EXECUTE LANE_MODEL=opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash LANE_EFFORT=high ROUTE_STYLE=openai \
  env TMPDIR="$LOGD" LANE_WT="$CLONE" CODEX_BIN="$STUB" bash "$L" NT14 brief_nt.md verify/nt >"$LOGD/T14.out" 2>&1; c=$?
ck T14_ROUTE_MISMATCH_REFUSED 6 "$c"
has T14_names_the_mismatch "route mismatch" "$LOGD/T14.out"

echo
echo "RESULT pass=$pass fail=$fail"
[ "$fail" -eq 0 ] || exit 1
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Negative tests for the model/effort admission gate (run_lanes_gated.sh).
# External clone only; the Codex binary is replaced by stubs, so nothing is launched
